        "speakerGender": "female",
        "transcript": "Today I want to walk you through what's actually happening inside a leaf in October, because the popular explanation is only about half right.\n\nLet's start with the pigments. All summer, the dominant pigment is chlorophyll, which absorbs red and blue light and reflects green. But chlorophyll is unstable. The tree has to keep rebuilding it, and that costs energy. Underneath it, the whole time, sit the carotenoids, the yellows and oranges. They were there in June. You just couldn't see them.\n\nSo as nights get longer, the tree begins shutting down. It grows a layer of corky cells at the base of the leaf stalk, and chlorophyll production stops. The green fades, and the yellow that was always present becomes visible.\n\nNow here's the part students routinely get backward. People assume all fall color works this way, that it's simply green wearing off. That's true for birches and hickories. But the reds, the ones in maples and sumacs, come from anthocyanins, and anthocyanins are manufactured fresh in the autumn. The tree spends energy to make them, which is strange behavior for an organ it's about to discard.\n\nTake a sugar maple on a hillside. The trees on the sunny south face turn brilliant scarlet; the same species in shade stays yellowish. Why? Bright light plus cool nights traps sugar in the leaf, and that sugar feeds anthocyanin production.\n\nOne leading hypothesis is sunscreen: the red pigment shields the leaf while the tree pulls nitrogen back into the branches. So color may be salvage, not decay.",
